# Summary
Introduced bug: POST Object request policy context omits the accepted website redirect form field, causing the newly supported s3:x-amz-website-redirect-location condition key to be evaluated against None/Absent for POST uploads.
The new condition key is registered and object authorization resolves it from PutObjectPolicyContext.website_redirect_location. Normal PUT paths populate that field from the x-amz-website-redirect-location header, but the POST Object path only copies the form field into metadata and then builds a PutObjectPolicyContext without calling with_website_redirect_location(field(...)). As a result, bucket-policy Deny statements such as StringEquals/StringLike on s3:x-amz-website-redirect-location see the value as absent for POST Object and do not match. A client that otherwise has PutObject permission, including a public-write or delegated POST upload flow, can upload an object with redirect metadata that the bucket policy was intended to forbid. The equivalent PUT request is correctly evaluated, so this is an introduced authorization gap in the new condition-key support.

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [be278936a5bf2fb2a61188ce650ee5b5fddf2b48](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/be278936a5bf2fb2a61188ce650ee5b5fddf2b48).

The fix adds AWS-facing coverage proving that `s3:x-amz-website-redirect-location`
is evaluated for POST Object form fields: a broad `PutObject` allow remains
successful for a nonmatching redirect value, while an explicit Deny on
`/docs/blocked.html` rejects the matching POST form field with `AccessDenied`.

The local POST Object path now passes the accepted
`x-amz-website-redirect-location` form field into `PutObjectPolicyContext` via
`with_website_redirect_location(field(WEBSITE_REDIRECT_LOCATION_HEADER_NAME))`,
matching the existing PUT path and preventing redirect metadata from bypassing
bucket-policy Deny conditions.

