## Report
Validated the finding with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC. The registered condition key is present at crates/auth/src/bucket_policy/condition_key.rs:355-361 and resolves via request.website_redirect_location() at condition_key.rs:640-645. Object authorization copies only input.policy_context.website_redirect_location into policy evaluation at crates/server-core/src/coordinator/authz/policy.rs:155-160. POST Object accepts the x-amz-website-redirect-location form field into parsed metadata at crates/server-http/src/http/mod.rs:3630-3647, but the POST authorization context built at http/mod.rs:3680-3692 does not call with_website_redirect_location(...). By contrast, the PUT path supplies req.header(WEBSITE_REDIRECT_LOCATION_HEADER_NAME) at http/mod.rs:4044-4052. I added a PoC test that installs a bucket-policy Deny for s3:x-amz-website-redirect-location == /blocked.html, confirms an equivalent signed PUT is denied, then submits a signed POST Object form with x-amz-website-redirect-location=/blocked.html. On the vulnerable commit, prepare_streaming_post_object returns Ok, so the PoC panics with BUG_REPRODUCED. Command output: `test http::tests::poc_post_object_website_redirect_policy_bypass ... FAILED` and panic message `POST Object ... bypassed the bucket-policy Deny that blocks the same PUT header`. I also verified the proposed one-line fix `.with_website_redirect_location(field(WEBSITE_REDIRECT_LOCATION_HEADER_NAME))` makes the same test pass. Valgrind and gdb were unavailable in the container; lldb was available and stopped at the PoC panic line with a backtrace showing POST reached the unexpected Ok branch.

